@node Lexical Conventions
@section Lexical Conventions

@subsubsection Keywords

Keywords are case-insensitive and matched via @code{string-equal}. The @code{@} prefixed
keywords are recognized only when the lexeme begins with @code{@}.

@multitable @columnfractions 0.4 0.3 0.3
@headitem Keyword @tab Token Symbol @tab Description
@item @code{@@interface} @tab :AT-INTERFACE @tab Class interface
@item @code{@@implementation} @tab :AT-IMPLEMENTATION @tab Class implementation
@item @code{@@end} @tab :AT-END @tab End of interface/implementation
@item @code{@@property} @tab :AT-PROPERTY @tab Property declaration
@item @code{@@selector} @tab :AT-SELECTOR @tab Selector reference
@item @code{@@protocol} @tab :AT-PROTOCOL @tab Protocol declaration
@item @code{@public} @tab :AT-PUBLIC @tab Public access
@item @code{@private} @tab :AT-PRIVATE @tab Private access
@item @code{@protected} @tab :AT-PROTECTED @tab Protected access
@item @code{@synthesize} @tab :AT-SYNTHESIZE @tab Property synthesis
@item @code{@try} @tab :AT-TRY @tab Exception try block
@item @code{@catch} @tab :AT-CATCH @tab Exception handler
@item @code{@finally} @tab :AT-FINALLY @tab Cleanup block
@item @code{@throw} @tab :AT-THROW @tab Raise exception
@item @code{@return} @tab :AT-RETURN @tab Return from method
@item @code{if} @tab :IF @tab Conditional
@item @code{else} @tab :ELSE @tab Alternative branch
@item @code{for} @tab :FOR @tab Counted loop
@item @code{while} @tab :WHILE @tab Pre-test loop
@item @code{do} @tab :DO @tab Post-test loop
@item @code{return} @tab :RETURN @tab Return statement
@item @code{break} @tab :BREAK @tab Exit loop
@item @code{continue} @tab :CONTINUE @tab Next iteration
@item @code{self} @tab :SELF @tab Current instance
@item @code{super} @tab :SUPER @tab Parent class
@item @code{id} @tab :ID-TYPE @tab Generic object type
@item @code{void} @tab :VOID @tab No return type
@item @code{DISPLAY} @tab :DISPLAY @tab Output statement
@item @code{ACCEPT} @tab :ACCEPT @tab Input statement
@item @code{SPEAK} @tab :SPEAK @tab Dialogue statement
@item @code{COPY} @tab :COPY @tab Copybook inclusion
@end multitable

@subsubsection Operators

@multitable @columnfractions 0.25 0.25 0.5
@headitem Operator @tab Token @tab Description
@item @code{+} @tab :PLUS @tab Addition
@item @code{-} @tab :MINUS @tab Subtraction
@item @code{*} @tab :TIMES @tab Multiplication
@item @item @code{/} @tab :DIVIDE @tab Division
@item @code{%} @tab :MODULO @tab Modulus
@item @code{=} @tab :EQUAL @tab Assignment
@item @code{==} @tab :EQUAL-EQUAL @tab Equality test
@item @code{!=} or @code{<>} @tab :NOT-EQUAL @tab Inequality test
@item @code{<} @tab :LT @tab Less than
@item @code{>} @tab :GT @tab Greater than
@item @code{<=} @tab :LE @tab Less or equal
@item @code{>=} @tab :GE @tab Greater or equal
@item @code{&&} @tab :AND-AND @tab Logical AND
@item @code{||} @tab :OR-OR @tab Logical OR
@item @code{!} @tab :NOT @tab Logical NOT
@item @code{[} @tab :LBRACKET @tab Message send start
@item @code{]} @tab :RBRACKET @tab Message send end
@item @code{:} @tab :COLON @tab Selector separator
@item @code{->} @tab :ARROW @tab Pointer dereference
@item @code{.} @tab :DOT @tab Property access
@item @code{+=} @tab :PLUS-EQUAL @tab Add and assign
@item @code{-=} @tab :MINUS-EQUAL @tab Subtract and assign
@item @code{*=} @tab :TIMES-EQUAL @tab Multiply and assign
@item @code{/=} @tab :DIVIDE-EQUAL @tab Divide and assign
@end multitable

@subsubsection Number Literals

The lexer supports multiple number formats with prefixes:

@table @asis
@item Decimal — No prefix required: @code{42}, @code{100}, @code{3}
@item Hexadecimal — Prefix @code{0x}: @code{0xFF}, @code{0xDEADBEEF}
@item Octal — Prefix @code{0o}: @code{0o755}, @code{0o644}
@item Binary — Prefix @code{0b}: @code{0b1010}, @code{0b11110000}
@item Dword — Prefix @code{0d"WORD"}: @code{0d"12345678"}
@end table

All numeric formats produce a @code{:NUMBER} token. The lexer validates digits according
to the specified radix.

@subsubsection String Literals

String literals are enclosed in double quotes: @code{"text"}. The lexer handles escaped
quotes within strings.

@example
"Hello, World!"
"Line one\nLine two"
@end example

@subsubsection Identifiers

Identifiers consist of alphanumeric characters and underscores, and must not begin with a
digit. All identifiers are normalized to PascalCase during lexing.

@node Class Definition
@section Class Definition

@menu
* @interface:: Class interface declaration
* @implementation:: Class implementation
* @end:: End of class definition
@end menu

@node @interface
@subsection @code{@@interface}

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{@@interface} declares the public interface of a class, including its superclass,
instance variables, and method declarations. The interface defines what operations are
available to other classes.

@subheading Syntax

@example
@@interface ClassName
@@end

@@interface ClassName : SuperClass
@@end

@@interface ClassName
@@property type name;
@@end
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
@@interface Player
@@end

@@interface GameSprite : Sprite
@@property int x, y;
@@property int health;
@@end
@end example

@subheading Valid Inputs

@itemize
@item ClassName must be a valid identifier
@item SuperClass is optional; defaults to NSObject or equivalent root
@item Property declarations follow within the interface body
@end itemize

@subheading Output

Maps to EightBol's class definition structure.

@node @implementation
@subsection @code{@@implementation}

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{@@implementation} provides the implementation of a class's methods. It typically
pairs with an @code{@@interface} declaration.

@subheading Syntax

@example
@@implementation ClassName
method implementations...
@@end
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
@@implementation Player
- (void)takeDamage:(int)amount {
    health = health - amount;
}
@@end
@end example

@node @end
@subsection @code{@@end}

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{@@end} terminates an @code{@@interface}, @code{@@implementation}, or
@code{@@protocol} block.

@subheading Syntax

@example
@@interface ClassName
    declarations...
@@end

@@implementation ClassName
    implementations...
@@end
@end example

@node Property Declaration
@section Property Declaration

@menu
* @property:: Declare a property
* @synthesize:: Generate accessor methods
* @dynamic:: Defer accessor generation
@end menu

@node @property
@subsection @code{@@property}

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{@@property} declares a property, which is an instance variable with automatic
getter and optionally setter accessor methods.

@subheading Syntax

@example
@@property type name;
@@property(type) name;
@@property(type, attributes) name;
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
@@property int score;
@@property(assign) int x, y;
@@property(retain) NSString *playerName;
@@property(copy) NSString *message;
@end example

@subheading Valid Attributes

@table @asis
@item @code{assign} — Simple assignment (default for scalar types)
@item @code{retain} — Retain the object on assignment
@item @code{copy} — Copy the object on assignment
@item @code{readonly} — Generate only a getter
@item @code{readwrite} — Generate both getter and setter (default)
@end table

@subheading Output

Maps to EightBol's data definition with appropriate usage and attributes.

@node @synthesize
@subsection @code{@@synthesize}

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{@@synthesize} instructs the compiler to automatically generate getter and setter
methods for a property.

@subheading Syntax

@example
@@synthesize propertyName;
@@synthesize propertyName = instanceVariableName;
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
@@synthesize health;
@@synthesize playerName = _playerName;
@end example

@node @dynamic
@subsection @code{@@dynamic}

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{@@dynamic} tells the compiler that accessor methods will be provided at runtime
(e.g., via @code{dlopen} or manual implementation).

@subheading Syntax

@example
@@dynamic propertyName;
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
@@dynamic health;
@end example

@node Method Declarations
@section Method Declarations

@menu
* Instance Methods::
* Class Methods::
* Message Send Syntax::
* self and super::
@end menu

@node Instance Methods
@subsection Instance Methods

@subheading Purpose and Function

Instance methods operate on a specific instance of a class. They are declared with a
leading hyphen.

@subheading Syntax

@example
- (returnType)methodName;
- (returnType)methodName:(paramType)paramName;
- (returnType)methodName:(paramType)param1 otherParam:(paramType)param2;
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
- (void)reset;
- (int)getHealth;
- (void)takeDamage:(int)amount;
- (int)calculateScore:(int)bonus multiplier:(int)factor;
@end example

@node Class Methods
@subsection Class Methods

@subheading Purpose and Function

Class methods operate on the class itself, not on instances. They are declared with a
leading plus sign.

@subheading Syntax

@example
+ (returnType)methodName;
+ (returnType)methodName:(paramType)paramName;
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
+ (Player *)sharedPlayer;
+ (void)initializeGame;
@end example

@node Message Send Syntax
@subsection Message Send Syntax

@subheading Purpose and Function

Message send syntax invokes a method on an object. It uses bracket notation with
colons to separate arguments.

@subheading Syntax

@example
[object method];
[object method:arg];
[object method:param1 other:param2];
[object method:param1 other:param2 another:param3];
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
[player reset];
[player takeDamage:10];
[game addSprite:sprite atX:100 y:200];
[calculator computeWithA:x andB:y operation:"add"];
@end example

@subheading Output

Maps to EightBol's @code{:method} or @code{:invoke} AST nodes.

@node self and super
@subsection @code{self} and @code{super}

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{self} refers to the current instance; @code{super} refers to the superclass
implementation.

@subheading Syntax

@example
[self methodName];
[super methodName];
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
- (void)reset {
    [super reset];
    health = 100;
}
@end example

@node Statements
@section Statements

@menu
* Variable Declaration::
* Assignment Statement::
* Return Statement::
* Expression Statement::
@end menu

@node Variable Declaration
@subsection Variable Declaration

@subheading Purpose and Function

Local variables are declared with the @code{local} keyword (EightBol style) or as simple
identifiers with assignment.

@subheading Syntax

@example
local variableName = value;
local variableName = { element1, element2, ... };
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
local x = 0;
local score = 100;
local colors = { 255, 0, 0, 255 };
@end example

@subheading Output

Maps to EightBol's @code{:dd} (data definition) node.

@node Assignment Statement
@subsection Assignment Statement

@subheading Purpose and Function

Assignment statements store a value into a variable.

@subheading Syntax

@example
variableName = expression;
variableName += expression;
variableName -= expression;
variableName *= expression;
variableName /= expression;
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
x = 42;
score = score + bonus;
health -= damage;
@end example

@subheading Output

Maps to EightBol's @code{:set} node.

@node Return Statement
@subsection Return Statement

@subheading Purpose and Function

@code{return} exits the current method and optionally returns a value.

@subheading Syntax

@example
return;
return expression;
@end example

@subheading Examples

@example
return;
return result;
return x + y;
@end example

@subheading Output

Maps to EightBol's @code{:exit-method} node.

@node Expression Statement
@subsection Expression Statement

@subheading Purpose and Function

Any expression can be used as a statement; its value is discarded.

@subheading Syntax

@example
functionName();
object method:arg;
@end example

